course: dictionary
lesson: 5109
title: Interval
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An interval is a gap or a space of time between two things. Think of it as the empty part between two events, like the time between a bell and the next bell. Read the words around it to find its meaning.
explain: «At intervals he faintly murmured "Poop-poop!"» Toad says the words now and then, with gaps between them, not all the time. So at intervals means from time to time, with breaks in between. Try your meaning in the sentence: "now and then" fits, and "all the time without stopping" does not.
explain: Badger «nodded gravely at intervals as the animals told their story.» He nods every so often while he listens, not once and not constantly. The phrase «at regular intervals» goes one step further: it means again and again with the same gap, like buses every ten minutes.
fact: An interval is a period of time between two events.
fact: An interval is a space or gap between two things or points.
fact: In the UK, an interval is a short break in the middle of a play, concert or show. In the US, this is called an intermission.
fact: In maths, an interval is the range of numbers between two values, or the step between marks on a scale, such as every 5 on a number line.
fact: «At regular intervals» means again and again with the same gap in between.
exercise: «Badger nodded gravely at intervals as the animals told their story.» What does at intervals mean here? | All the time without stopping | *Now and then, with gaps between | Never once | Very quickly
exercise: On a number line the marks are placed at equal intervals of 5. What is the interval? | 0 | *5 | 50 | 1
exercise: In a theatre, what is the interval? | The end of the show | The first song | *A short break in the middle of the show
test: Buses leave the station at regular intervals, every ten minutes. What does regular intervals mean? | *Again and again with the same gap | Never | At random, with no pattern | Only once a day
test: The time between two events is called what? | A sunny day | A placid mood | *An interval | An axis
exam: In data handling, class intervals such as 10–19 and 20–29 group the results. What is a class interval? | One single result | A type of graph paper | *A group of numbers in a range | A break in a concert
